course: dictionary
lesson: 6414
title: Muslim as a noun and an adjective
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Muslim can be a noun, which names a person, or an adjective, which describes something. A Muslim is a person who follows the religion of Islam. Muslim can also describe things, as in «a Muslim family» or «Muslim festival».
explain: Look at the words around it to see which job it does. In «a Muslim family», the word family is the thing, and Muslim tells us what kind of family it is. So here Muslim is an adjective. Ask yourself: is this word a person, or is it describing one?
explain: Muslims believe in one God, called Allah, and follow the teachings of the Prophet Muhammad and the Qur'an. Their place of prayer is the mosque. When you see the word in a lesson about religions, it is usually about these beliefs and customs.
fact: A Muslim is a person who follows the religion of Islam.
fact: As an adjective, Muslim means to do with Islam or Muslims, as in «Muslim festivals».
fact: Muslims believe in one God, called Allah, and follow the Prophet Muhammad and the Qur'an.
fact: Muslims pray five times a day, often in a mosque, and fast during the month of Ramadan.
fact: Festivals include Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. Many Muslims eat halal food and no pork.
exercise: In «a Muslim family», what kind of word is Muslim? | Verb, because it shows an action | Noun, because it names a person | *Adjective, because it describes the family
exercise: Which word names the place where Muslims pray? | Station | Library | Market | *Mosque
exercise: Which of these is a Muslim festival? | Hanukkah | Christmas | *Eid al-Fitr | Diwali
test: What is the name of the one God that Muslims believe in? | *Allah | Ra | Zeus | Thor
test: How many times a day do Muslims pray? | *Five times | Once | Twice | Ten times
exam: In the phrase «Muslim festivals», what kind of word is Muslim? | Noun | Verb | *Adjective | Adverb
